{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"enstatite-organic-geese","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Review-bot Gerrit comment truncates findings silently (no ellipsis, no \"N more\" note)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nFound incidentally while investigating ticket 368a (Gerrit line breaks — closed as not_a_bug). NOT the reported bug there; filed separately.\n\n`src/rebar/review_bot/adapter.py:117-125` builds the blocking-finding comment the review bot posts on a Gerrit `-1`. Two silent truncations:\n\n1. `adapter.py:122` — `detail = str(f.get(\"finding\", \"\")).strip().replace(\"\\n\", \" \")[:240]`. A finding longer than 240 chars is clipped mid-sentence with NO ellipsis or marker, so the author cannot tell the text was cut. Verified by execution: a 690-char finding produced a 271-char line ending mid-token with no `…`.\n2. `adapter.py:120` — `for f in blocking[:10]`. With more than 10 blocking findings the rest are dropped with no \"N more\" note, so the comment understates how much must be fixed.\n\nThis matters because the comment is the actionable artifact for a blocked change: a truncated finding can be unactionable, and a hidden overflow reads as \"that's all of them\".\n\n## Why this is a defect\n\nThe codebase has its own convention for marking truncation: `src/rebar/review_bot/voter.py:440` emits an explicit `… n unchanged line(s) …` marker. The same module truncating without a marker is inconsistent with that established pattern. The rebar-debug protocol's own guidance is the general principle: a bounded output must not read as complete when it is not.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A finding clipped at the length cap ends with an explicit marker (e.g. ` …`) so the reader knows text was removed.\n- When more than the per-comment cap of blocking findings exist, the comment states how many were omitted (e.g. \"… and N more\").\n- The full detail remains discoverable (the cap stays — the point is disclosure, not raising the limit).\n- Existing review-bot comment tests keep passing; the marker is asserted by a new test.\n\n## Notes\n\nLow priority / UX. Confirmed by reading `adapter.py:117-125` and by executing the formatter with an over-length finding. Real posted bot comments observed in the wild are short (<=63 chars), so this triggers only on verbose blocking findings.","id":"8f85-f170-1413-4040","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Review-bot Gerrit comment truncates findings with no ellipsis or overflow note"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785219727674941001,"uuid":"2eca7ad0-22a9-4bea-a0b8-46cfe607648a"}